# A short proof of the existence of designs

Entry point for the formalization of Peter Keevash's paper
*A short proof of the existence of designs* (arXiv:2411.18291).

## Main theorem and the original explicit threshold

`design_existence_paper_threshold` proves, for every `q > r ≥ 1` and
`n ≥ paperSizeThreshold q r`, that a divisible complete `r`-graph on
`Fin n` has an actual `K_q^r`-decomposition. The threshold is exactly

`(4*q)^(90*q*(2*q)^r*(6*choose(q,r))^2)`.

The equivalent numerical criterion is
`hasDecomposition_iff_binomial_divisibility_paper_threshold`: each
`choose(q-i,r-i)` divides `choose(n-i,r-i)` for `i ≤ r`. The proof
constructs the reserve, sparse absorber, regular clique family, nibble
packing, and cover. Rank one is handled by a partition. No construction,
generation, growth, or probability estimate is assumed.

The earlier qualitative `design_existence` and the sufficient bound
`correctedDesignThreshold` remain available. That older bound includes a
conservative flattening cost and is larger than the printed n0 for
triangles. This is a limitation of the older route, not of the new theorem.

## Sparse absorption without a fixed multiplicity cap

`exists_sparse_absorber_paper_threshold` proves the full sparse absorber
lemma at n0 in every positive rank. Its host is n^(-alpha/4)-bounded and
absorbs every divisible subgraph of the given n^(-rho)-bounded source.
In rank one, every divisible graph already decomposes, so the empty host
is an absorber. For the other ranks the proof uses the following chain.

The typical host and modular generator family have simultaneous face and
edge caps. A relative error n^(-alpha/60), squared saturation loss, and
explicit logarithmic colour trials construct the required rainbow
extensions and modular generation. Repeating colour labels does not
enlarge the generator union. All good-density powers through the full
exchange size retain half their reference values.

For q at least three, the full logarithmic palette coefficient plus one
is at most n^(alpha/20). Focusing has edge multiplicity at most one,
and local decoding adds at most choose(q,r). Integral lifting therefore
constructs generators with edge multiplicity at most n^(alpha/10), using
half the n^(-3alpha/5) boundary-degree budget. The source and generator
support together satisfy the full budget.

Weighted decoder concentration uses increment cap 1+n^(alpha/10) and
constant deviation. The decoder coefficient is at most (4q)^(3q), giving
both graph and capacity density n^(-17alpha/30). Variable-capacity
splitting produces one fixed signed family for every admissible leave,
with graph density n^(-alpha/2), clique-boundary density
n^(-89alpha/180), and edge multiplicity at most n^(7alpha/60).

Writing R for the edge rank, k=choose(q,R), H for the elimination
pattern size, and theta=n^(-17alpha/45), each indexed near-pair coordinate
has face degrees below theta*n. First cancellation retains the raw
graph bound C1*theta, where C1=1+4*R!*H, as well as n^(-alpha/3).

Each bad negative clique has a unique positive far partner. A far
positive clique is used at most 4*k*n^(7alpha/60) times, because its
edges lie outside the original splitting graph, where multiplicity is
at most two. The second root degrees and existing graph are therefore
bounded by A2*theta, where A2=4*k+2*(q-R+1)+2*C1. The bounds
C1 ≤ (4q)^(4q) and A2 ≤ (4q)^(5q) give a constructed second elimination
family of density n^(-5alpha/18), stronger than n^(-alpha/4).

Both selected cancellation stages preserve the exact boundary and
disjoint signs. The final negative family is a true decomposition of a
fixed host disjoint from the source. Adding its unused cliques converts
every signed leave into a true decomposition of the host plus that leave.

## Other completed results and source corrections

The formalization includes the explicit local decoder and coefficient
bound; degree divisibility on all n ≥ q+r vertices; the complete exchange
configuration with carrier bounds, frame locality, and cross simplicity;
and all deterministic decomposition and absorption identities.
Reserve, Cover, Boost, and Lemma 2.4's nibble are proved at n0 with the
paper's parameters. Boost already holds at n ≥ (4q)^(90q).

The signed version of Lemma 5.1(1) is false. A finite independent
counterexample is formalized, and the authorized correction adds
nonnegative summands. Part 2 holds as stated for bounded adapted variables.
The ordinary and stopped greedy processes are constructed, with equal
bounded-success probabilities and explicit adaptive concentration bounds.
The literal process definition wrongly forbids prescribed root edges in B;
a nontrivial two-vertex example formally refutes it for every n≥1025.
The implemented process exempts roots. In rank one its full printed
smallness bound gives success with probability one, retaining the input
degree bound, whenever n≥2*v_H. No lower density cutoff is needed there.

The printed exp(-n/10) high-probability convention is also false for
Lemma 5.3. Its counterexample uses ordinary graphs with p=1/100. The
corrected failure below exp(-n^(1/10)) holds at the source's original local
threshold n ≥ 2^(9*R*h), in every positive rank. No small-parameter
cases remain. KSG has all three source conclusions at n0 for the paper's
fixed modulus N=R!*choose(q,R), introduced in the local-decoder lemma and
used again in the integral lift. `paper_modular_generators_whp` states this
without an extra modulus hypothesis. The extension to every positive
modulus holds at an explicit modulus-dependent threshold; it equals n0
through the decoder modulus. Arbitrary larger moduli at unchanged n0 are
not an additional claim made by the paper.

All four original colour conclusions hold jointly at n0 in every positive
rank with the printed palette and failure at most n^(-1). The four actual
dependent absorber placement laws also have a proved joint failure bound
exp(-n^(1/10)) at n0 after supplying multiplicity-16 integral generators.
The new capped route above proves unconditional existence without this
fixed-multiplicity input.

The multiplicity-two conclusions of Lemmas 6.1 and 6.5 are false for
choose(q,r)>2, even on sparse inputs at arbitrarily large sizes. The
counterexamples and a multiplicity-16 replacement at a larger explicit
threshold are proved. The new main proof does not require flattening.

Section 9's quantitative nibble now holds at n0 with the exact printed
constant 3, for every epsilon ≤ 2/5 in all positive ranks.
`exists_sparse_nibble_constant_three_all_positive_ranks` constructs an
actual packing from the original degree regularity and density assumptions.
No extra size threshold or exceptional parameter range remains. The earlier
eventual constant-3 result also holds throughout epsilon<1/2.

For clique sizes k=3,4,5, logarithmic degree and count comparisons give
the remaining finite construction. All parameter, step/window, failure,
and rounded-horizon estimates are proved at n0. Stopping at (5/2)*p gives
leave density at most (5/2)*p+3*p^k ≤ 3*p for p≤1/3; larger p permits
the empty packing. The pair case and scaled comparisons for k≥6 complete
the proof in every positive rank.

The standalone decoder/focusing lemma also holds at n0 with its exact
printed boundary coefficient 2^(q+2)*(4q)^R*u*n^(-7alpha/10).
Each input clique shares one randomly enlarged (q+R)-vertex region;
all its q-subsets are retained. The simultaneous face bounds give every
input edge a full local decoder region while using at most half the final
coefficient. Independent prescribed choices supply focusing cliques for
every reserve edge, including edges already in the coloured host. The
construction needs no disjointness between the reserve and coloured host,
and no upper bound on the input palette coefficient.

## Greedy smallness correction

The printed general greedy smallness condition is false even for the
intended corrected process. `arbitrarily_large_greedy_linear_counterexamples`
uses a fixed rank-two pattern on 257 vertices and satisfies every printed
input bound at arbitrarily large n, yet the success event is empty for
all output degree constants. Balanced rotations of pairwise-intersecting
root stars force more distinct centres than the ambient vertex count.
The proved quadratic smallness condition suffices for every application
in the paper; the main theorem and sparse absorber retain the original n0.

See `Arxiv2411_18291/PROGRESS.md` for the complete module inventory,
source-label coverage, verification commands, and assumption audit.
No proof uses placeholders, added axioms, unsafe evaluation, or increased
heartbeats or other computational limits.
